castle point and rochford health and  well-being strategy reset  mental health workshop from the 9th  september 2020  facilitated by sam ball who is from the  ccg  mental health commissioning team  three key questions to pull out from a  clinical commissioning perspective the  ccg is geographically aligned to  southend castle point and rockford  there are three things that would be  really valuable to pull out to help  enrich the mental health part of the  strategy  one what is the character of castle  point and rochford  two who are the key players who are  doing things that are making a  difference around mental health  that people are working in mental health  would need to know  for example the way volunteers were  mobilized during the covert 19 pandemic  this tells us something about the  community fabric these things are assets  that help people stay well and prevent  them becoming mentally ill in the first  place  three who are the unheard groups who out  there is not okay despite on  average castle point of rockford being  socioeconomically okay  and health opportunity wise being  relatively okay  mental health strategic drivers the nhs  long-term plan speaks about thresholds  to access secondary care and mental  health services  getting higher and higher this is owing  to services being organized in silos  driven by a diagnosis rather than need  and because of demand outstripping  resource  the long-term plan talks about how we  can change that and create more capacity  with investment to transform mental  health services  integration this is freeway between  health and social care but also between  mental health and physical health  primary care networks and social  prescribing link workers  these are our locality delivery  framework around which to hang services  community voluntary sector there are  huge opportunities as we transform  mental health services  to have a more enhanced role for the  community and voluntary sector  there are also local priorities these  are explained on the next slide  local priorities for a health and social  care lens  reflect those nationally there are high  thresholds for secondary care and siloed  pathways  for example in castle point and rochford  for every five referrals a gp will make  to a secondary mental health care  only four will end up being offered a  secondary mental health service  [ __ ] explained that this speaks to a  number of things really high thresholds  lack of capacity it being difficult for  gps to know where to place people  which means wasted time for primary and  secondary care practitioners  and a poor service user experience  large numbers of people managed in  primary care  the effects of kovin 19 and anticipated  surge  plans to address these integrated  primary community care placing senior  mental health practitioners  in each pcn in south east essex to  support those who don't meet eligibility  criteria  for improved access to psychological  therapies  also known as iapt or the threshold for  secondary health services  sambal commented on this saying we are  placing nine senior mental health  practitioners  one in each primary care network in  southeast essex  to meet the needs of people who fall  between iap and secondary care  redesigning provisions so that is more  integrated with primary care so that it  is more  needs-led and holistic an enhanced  therapeutic offer in the recovery  college  and wellbeing hubs creation of more  coherent preventative offers to support  patient flow  by allowing people to step down from  secondary care and create capacity  as well as changing the way services  look  other determinants to consider are  economic  lifestyle activity and family and social  what else do we need to consider what's  the 2019 jsna profiles tell us  from the 2019 joint strategic needs  assessment for castle point and rockford  we know that carlson point and rochford  ctg has the third highest prevalence  at 12.47 of depression and anxiety  in people over 18 compared across that  of the other  ccgs in essex this is similar to the  prevalence across  essex at 12.49 and lower than that  across england at 13.74 percent  21 of residents aged 16 to 65 plus  reported feeling  isolated from others this was the lowest  percentage compared to that of the other  districts in essex  and lower compared to ethics as a whole  at 25.5 percent  sam ball said that what this really  tells us is that castle point and  rockford  does not particularly stand out we are  not a particular outlier according to  the statistics  but that doesn't mean it isn't a problem  for individuals  approximately 0.6 percent of the  population of rochford  and 0.7 of the population of castle  point have a severe mental health  condition  such as schizophrenia bipolar affective  disorder  psychotic depression or another  psychotic illness  as recorded on general practice disease  registers  in 2017 to 18. this is below the essex  average at 0.8  an england average of 0.94  the estimated prevalence of any mental  health disorder in children and young  people aged 5 to 16 years  is 8.89 in castle point and 8.24 in  rochford  both are below the england value at 9.23  the castle point is higher than the  average prevalence across essex as a  whole  at 8.71  improved access to psychological  therapies also known as iapt  therapy for you is the nhs commission's  psychological therapy service  used to treat common mental health  conditions like depression  and anxiety disorders in south essex  therapy for use is roughly 5 000 to 6  000 people a year alone in castle point  and rockford  and more than 50 of those recovered from  their depression or anxiety  covered 19 has had a significant impact  on the number of people being referred  to iact in the last six months  in quarter one of this year april may  and june referrals to iaps fell well  below the target numbers  but has surged back up already beyond  the planned levels  sample explained that iapt is a busy  effective and well organized service  around quarter one of this year people  stopped coming to the services  they stopped seeking help for mental  health problems but it has surged back  up already  age and gender comparisons men are  underrepresented in iact  women fire outweigh men seeking help in  terms of psychological  therapy people over the age of 65 are  also underrepresented in iat  sambal explained that when we think  about early intervention and prevention  and the well-being of the population we  need to consider these facts  and how we make it okay for men and  older people to reach out for help  sometimes it can be generational that  people aren't comfortable having that  kind of conversation  we need to address this and reduce that  stigma  questions to consider these are the  questions we need to consider  one what does this data tell us two what  do you think  are the key priorities free how should  we be addressing them  let's also revert to the other three  questions mentioned at the beginning  one what is the character of castle  point and rochford  two who are the key players doing key  things that will help with mental health  and well-being  three who are the unheard groups and  individuals  who might not show on the statistics  because generally  in castle point and rockford things are  okay  phe prevention concordat this is a  national movement focusing on the  promotion of good mental health and  prevention of  mental health issues at a local level it  aims to facilitate local action  creating resilient communities by taking  a prevention-focused approach  our health and well-being board has  developed a series of shared commitments  focusing on cross-section action  over five key areas of work to become a  signatory  our commitment plan will be submitted to  public health england when the next  submission waves open late this year  this date is to be confirmed sam ball  commented saying  this is a commitment for everyone to  pull together and is a key part of the  health and wellbeing strategy  for caster point of rockford it speaks  to the opportunity to be more  preventative  and think about those community assets  and key players  comments from the discussion matt king  said the investment at the moment is in  primary care and mental health  and we at trustlinks are just about to  pilot a support worker role  based with the mental health nurse in  the canva pcn  this will be a voluntary sector support  worker who will provide a bridge through  to the voluntary community sector  services  and also provides some targeted  interventions for those who are  struggling to access services  perhaps aren't at the levels whose need  secondary mental health services  nick chard said looking at the current  strategy it only talks about the severe  mental health disorders  we talk about more demand than resource  available and prevention  that high level mental health is only  the tip of the iceberg  we need to make sure we include  preventative love of tears i.e community  therapy and statutory iaps services  which prevent people reaching that high  level mental health issue in the first  place  candice mcpherson said i do feel that  the early intervention and prevention  element is missing in the current  strategy  it is quite high level but what about  everything else  sam ball said building a prevention  pathway is an explicit  objective and pulling out the role of  the voluntary sector and having a  clearer bigger role for the voluntary  sector  is an explicit plan the feedback is that  we need to reflect that in the health  and wellbeing strategy  sambal also said regarding the point  about social isolation  is there a specific need for a  population because of how rural it is  are there people of transitional age who  are in danger of missing out on services  because of where they live  matt king responded to this saying with  kovid we've all gone digital  so people are accessing more if they are  digitally literate  but there is digital isolation too if  people don't have access to the internet  or aren't very good with technology  transport to access services  is also an issue particularly in  rochefort districts like great wakering  and for some south end becomes your main  center  because of the geography and where the  town is the issue with canby is the  issue of access off the island to access  services and also greater levels of  deprivation  and intergenerational deprivation on  canva we also need to mention young  people's mental health  even in affluent areas is a real issue  school pressure is placed on children to  perform and achieve  self-harming anorexia and anxiety is  probably a ticking time ball  nick chard said the children with  anxiety now are the mental health  patients of the future so we need to  make sure they are properly spotted now  to prevent  that future need candy's mcpherson said  my role is specifically more focused on  early years and young people  and i do find that i have to really push  that best start in life element  of the health and well-being strategy so  i think that is something to consider  counselor johnson said the problem with  the strategy is how deep do we  go in a document that makes it still  readable and something that  someone can pick up and understand my  fear is that if we start delving into  something too deep  then this becomes uninteresting for the  people who need this  and need to understand what is out there  for them the key themes from this  conversation are  isolation be that social rural or  digital  the role of the voluntary sector greater  emphasis on early years  and children and young people's mental  health  next steps to meet before the next board  meeting to continue discussion and  develop the narrative around this  priority  for our new strategy is there other  intelligence you can share to inform and  provide more insight  who needs to be involved and how what  other actions would you consider  necessary to help drive forward our  prevention  concordat commitment plan

